      <node id="113320419551450266" label="#NorthCarolinaNorthCarolina#HurricaneHeleneHurricaneHeleneSome mountain towns in North Carolina are still in the dark after Hurricane Helene. And now, cold temperatures and snow are coming.By Jesse FerrellPublished Oct 15, 2024&quot;Winterlike weather has come early and will leave western North Carolina residents shivering, including thousands still without power nearly three weeks after Hurricane Helene. Tuesday night, light snow fell above 3,000 feet in elevation at Maggie Valley, Waynesville, and at the Beech Mountain, Cataloochee, and Appalachian Mountain ski areas.&quot;Snow also likely fell at the top of Mount Mitchell Tuesday night. The mountain is still without power, and its webcam and weather station have been offline since Helene hit on Sept. 27.&quot;Nearly three weeks ago, Hurricane Helene made landfall in Florida and went on to cause record flooding in the southern Appalachians. At least 232 people were killed by the storm. The storm knocked out power to 4.8 million customers. Although most power has been restored, for the last week, the number of homes and businesses still in the dark has remained steady at around 15,000 in three mountain counties in North Carolina, according to PowerOutage.US.&quot;Most of those still without power are in Mitchell County and Yancey County, home to Mount Mitchell, the highest peak east of the Mississippi River, nestled halfway between Asheville and Boone. Each county still has one-third of customers in the dark.&quot;AccuWeather Regional Expert John Feerick says the first truly cold air of the season will continue to plunge southward through the Appalachians this week. Frost and freeze alerts are in effect for most of the state's western counties.&quot;Asheville's flooding made most of the headlines, but the mountain town of Boone, in Wautagua County, was left almost completely without power and 'almost unrecognizable' after #HurricaneMilton, one resident told WXII last weekend. Citizens are worried that they will be forgotten.#HurricaneMiltonHurricaneMilton&quot;WataugaOnline said Monday that a curfew was still in effect for Boone and Blowing Rock overnight, and parts of the county 'have a great deal of road work, power restoration, and debris removal ongoing.'&quot;Snow after a hurricane is unusual but not rare. Some hurricanes, such as #HurricaneSandy, have even caused snow in the southern Appalachians.&quot;#HurricaneSandyHurricaneSandyhttps://www.accuweather.com/en/weather-news/snow-falls-on-north-carolinas-forgotten-mountain-towns-after-hurricane-helene/1704093https://www.accuweather.com/en/weather-news/snow-falls-on-north-carolinas-forgotten-mountain-towns-after-hurricane-helene/1704093https://www.accuweather.com/en/weather-news/snow-falls-on-north-carolinas-forgotten-mountain-towns-after-hurricane-helene/1704093">
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="0" value="2024-10-17 02:32:51+00:00" />
          <attvalue for="1" value="DoomsdaysCW@kolektiva.social" />
          <attvalue for="2" value="3" />
          <attvalue for="3" value="0" />
          <attvalue for="4" value="0" />
          <attvalue for="5" value="root" />
          <attvalue for="6" value="https://kolektiva.social/@DoomsdaysCW/113320419097192254" />
          <attvalue for="7" value="1.0" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="113318961989184829" label=" is mostly gone now. But the people are not. And, just to let everyone know...we are getting plenty of help. FEMA is here, as is a massive swarm of volunteers bringing all manner of aid--in the form of food, water, and various cleanup services. Our driveway was destroyed. Someone with heavy equipment and gravel showed up and repaired it. They did not ask to be paid--they just went to work and did it. A group of aid workers has set up a water distribution at the fire station that is providing WATER FOR OUR HORSES--meaning, 20+ gallons a day. My neighbor had a huge tree down (from our pasture) blocking his drive. Someone official (not sure who it was) arrived and removed it. There are people everywhere zooming about on ATVs knocking on doors delivering gas, food, water--whatever. The sky is filled with helicopters. This place was wrecked, and is now being rebuilt, as if by an army of ants coming from all directions. I know there is no compensating for the suffering, and in some cases, the loss of lives. But if we felt terrified and alone for the first week, that's over now. I look at this situation and think, well, it would be hard for anyone around here to be hungry right now. There is free food everywhere. You can walk up to the Baptist church and people are handing you barbecue sandwiches potato salad and when you leave they give you a bag of non-perishable food like peanut butter crackers, chocolate, fruit cups, etc. We don't need the help anymore so B has been volunteering and looking after others. If anyone says FEMA didn't come here they are ignorant of the facts. FEMA is basically omnipresent, at the moment. Don't give any credence to the lies about there being no help for us. There is help. In abundance. Thanks be to God.«Source:  https://www.facebook.com/share/p/yJt9TFY2dSxFvUYy/https://www.facebook.com/share/p/yJt9TFY2dSxFvUYy/https://www.facebook.com/share/p/yJt9TFY2dSxFvUYy/#Hurricane #Helene #Swannanoa #NC #NorthCarolina #FEMA #HurricaneHelene#HurricaneHurricane#HeleneHelene#SwannanoaSwannanoa#NCNC#NorthCarolinaNorthCarolina#FEMAFEMA#HurricaneHeleneHurricaneHelene">
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="0" value="2024-10-16 20:22:15+00:00" />
          <attvalue for="1" value="anti_disease@zeroes.ca" />
          <attvalue for="2" value="23" />
          <attvalue for="3" value="2" />
          <attvalue for="4" value="3" />
          <attvalue for="5" value="root" />
          <attvalue for="6" value="https://zeroes.ca/@anti_disease/113318961835248013" />
          <attvalue for="7" value="1.0" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
