      <node id="113275531331933580" label="ee.#StarlinkStarlink#HurricaneHeleneHurricaneHeleneA) Not surprisingly, you need a terminal. That's going to be a (discounted but non-refundable) cost of US$299 – but more like US$400 after shipping and tax. Oh, and it'll probably take 3 weeks to a month to turn up, assuming a UPS driver can drop it off. If the latter is true, you probably won't even need it by then.B) You get the free 30 days, but you need to commit to an additional month at US$120.There is evidence that there are Starlink terminals being used to provide free public wifi (over small areas, like any wifi) – but it appears that these aren’t being setup by Starlink but are instead private organisations and local government.It's basically a marketing opportunity for #Starlink and Musk, not a true humanitarian venture. I mean, they could be arranging for temporary Starlink-based free wifi-hubs themselves, possibly with plans to move them on to other disaster zones (e.g. #HurricaneMilton in Florida next week). But that would actually require _wanting_ to help out.#StarlinkStarlink#HurricaneMiltonHurricaneMiltonhttps://www.theregister.com/2024/10/08/free_starlink_hurricane_helene/https://www.theregister.com/2024/10/08/free_starlink_hurricane_helene/https://www.theregister.com/2024/10/08/free_starlink_hurricane_helene/">
