      <node id="113254562648581579" label="t of disorganised rainmaker storm (not hurricane level) dropping a lot of rain over Florida almost before the #HurricaneHelene search and rescue got started. Overnight (US time) they're showing a major (cat 3+) hurricane whacking the Florida peninsula - and only four days out. Models are generally pretty accurate that far ahead. Looks pretty bad already, but if the track goes north of Tampa Bay, it could be that the imaginary Tampa shields (ie., luck) finally ran out. Helene pushed 7-8 ft of surge into the bay, which broke records and did a lot of damage, but made landfall much further north. #HurricaneHeleneHurricaneHeleneHrmmm, perhaps Helene did FL a favour -- with so many houses already badly damaged, hopefully there aren't that many people around who need to evacuate...I'm too lazy to rant about the barking mad US system's tendency to push millions of people to live in some of the most dangerously exposed places this side of Bangladesh, or #climateChange / crisis, or how nutsoid insurance, housing bubble, wilful refusal to countenance managed retreat etc etc) exposes not just the US, but much of the rest of the world to enormous risk, so just imagine it. Or read some of Dr Masters' pieces in the Yale Climate Connections archive. Here's a good start -- https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2024/06/the-u-s-is-nowhere-near-ready-for-climate-change/#climateChangeclimateChangehttps://yaleclimateconnections.org/2024/06/the-u-s-is-nowhere-near-ready-for-climate-change/https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2024/06/the-u-s-is-nowhere-near-ready-for-climate-change/#Tampa #TampaFlooding #hurricane#TampaTampa#TampaFloodingTampaFlooding#hurricanehurricane">
