      <node id="113234494866249070" label=" outdoor skillz (aimed at Australians).#HurricaneHeleneHurricaneHeleneRead. Watch. Learn. Don’t look away. Don't be mean.Ultimately, ask yourself this. Could you survive an emergency? Not tomorrow, not next month, not after you’ve ordered a metric fuck-tonne of gear online.Now. With what you already have.Could you be self-reliant? Could you manage without diverting resources away from someone in greater need?Emergency warning systems are fallible. Weather is unpredictable.I highly recommend having a few items in case of emergency. Nothing flash. Nothing expensive. We humans don’t need much to survive. Air. Water. Food.💧 Water is life. Put a 10L goon sack of water in the back of the cupboard. Less than $4. The goon sack can be reused and it’s more portable than a hard container and will take up less space. Grab a cheap Kmart 15L dry sack to put the goon sack in. Grab a few while you’re there. If you’re in a flood situation you want your kit dry.🥫 Have a stash of non-perishables you can eat without cooking. Periodically check expiry dates and rotate.📻 Grab a wind-up emergency radio/torch/beacon. Radio is often the only way to receive information in an emergency. A wind-up radio requires no power source.For longer outages:💧 Consider water filtration for the long haul. I have a Lifestraw which I use to filter and boil water to make hot drinks and wash my food utensils.🔥 Some kind of stove but be mindful of the availability of fuel/fuel type.If you're looking for other stuff about emergency preparedness, check out my 🧵 :https://mastodon.au/@Jacquetta/113071235159419935https://mastodon.au/@Jacquetta/113071235159419935https://mastodon.au/@Jacquetta/113071235159419935">
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="0" value="2024-10-01 22:21:10+00:00" />
          <attvalue for="1" value="Jacquetta@mastodon.au" />
          <attvalue for="2" value="7" />
