      <node id="113269139720265666" label="putresible” in regulatory language. Food, dead animals, and other waste that are capable of putrefaction (putrescible) must be disposed of weekly or twice-weekly with whatever else is defined as “garbage” locally to avoid odor and fly breeding. I didn’t know that there was any specific smelly chemical associated with the term. Non-putrescible solid wastes may be disposed of less frequently and are covered by many terms that may differ by locale—the #USPHS used to use “garbage” for any mixture of waste that included putrescibles, “rubbish” for non-putrescible waste, “municipal solid waste” for either from residential &amp; commercial sources, “debris” for various kinds of inert wastes, and “refuse” or solid wastes for all of it. Flood wastes are a special case, since no matter what it started out as, it’s been contaminated by a mix of putrescible and toxic wastes of largely unknown types and concentration. #Helene #Cleanup@karlauerbach@karlauerbachkarlauerbach@compoundchem@compoundchemcompoundchem#USPHSUSPHS#HeleneHelene#CleanupCleanup">
        <attvalues>
