      <node id="113260166069231527" label="ement excavations, etc.) be hauled out of coastal areas, but require that any of it that isn’t crushed and reused now should be stockpiled for building dikes, raising highways, and using more fill for new construction as sea levels rise. This is especially true for areas in hurricane paths. Appalachia and much of the flooded areas may not have to worry about high tides, but with 100- or 1000-year rain events every decade you’re going to want a lot of fill. Try to get local governments to keep inert rubble from buildings, highways, and bridges separate from other demo materials so it can be used for reconstruction now or in the future. We’re all going to have to #retreat from parts of the coast and tidal river basins, but we don’t want to give up historic downtowns or our best farmland. We might replace the Golden Gate with a canal and locks—others will have to fight for New Orleans and the Keys. #helene #SeaLevelRise@ai6yr@ai6yrai6yr#retreatretreat#helenehelene#SeaLevelRiseSeaLevelRise">
