      <node id="113266865772576893" label=" it is not what you're used to. To carry it to its logical conclusion, you'll have to start running off of smaller things. Smaller water heaters smaller refrigerators built for RVs. Small water pump and off-grid systems to help stabilize your dwelling temperature as much as anything else. For today though, we start at the beginning with the power system. First things first get a 200 W solar panel, bonus points if it works when it's kind of cloudy or is double-sided and reinforced.Also get a power bank The more capacity the better. You will keep this later on. You can carry them around, they have handles.Congratulations! You're not ready to start. Why you have this is to learn first of all because there's going to be a bit of that. Second, it's cuz if you're doing this it's because you probably need it or at the very least have noticed that everything is on fire all the time, now you have it. How does that feel huh? This gives you basic power for cell phones and maybe even a brief burst of like a television or something. Maybe 4-8 hours.You can absolutely plug a satellite uplink into this, and it will absolutely do exactly what you're thinking right now *smiles*So now that we got that here it comes time for the big money, This will cost you. What seems like a fortune and it is. I mean for most of us it is not cheap. The reason it is not cheap is because you don't have to do it again. At least not for like a really long time. It is measured in decades. Do you spend more than $5,000 a decade on your power bill right now? How do to it then? Start off with the basic system. I'm not going to lie to you. You will want to die with this limited amount of electricity. The good news is getting started does not cost $5,000. Most of us expand these systems over time and if you avoid costly mistakes, it will save you a lot of everything. You need to just start out with a 48 volt system unless you live in a van and even then you probably still should consider it. Long story short, a 12 volt system maxes out at around 1,000 W because the amperage is too high. It will not go down and it is usually at 100. That is insane. If you do this eventually you will surely die. What you want is a 24 to 48 volt pure sign wave inverter. Power efficiency if you can get 95%. That's amazing. I would get one of those in a heartbeat but this one I promise you is not. This one's loud and it's heavy and it looks like it fell out of space and that's fine. What it is is 48 volts and reliable. Inverters run $250 to $500 Next is the solar array. Systems wired in parallel, increase the voltage and if you understand the math you can easily do this. I'll show you how. 12 volts times 4 is 48. That means that if you have things that are 12 volts like 12 volt panels or 12 volt batteries. If you wire sets of four of them in parallel,  The voltage goes up while the amperage stays the same. Series is the opposite. Of course it increases the amps.The next is buying things that are 24 volts already. 24 volts times 2 is also 48.What I did was just seed sets of solar panels that would add up to 48 volts. I have some that are 12 and some that are 24. It limits the system doing it like this, you should just do nothing but 24V+ panels and batteries.Doing this again. I would again skip 12 volt entirely and would put nothing but these 400 W 24 volt panels out there.It is cheaper to just buy larger panelsThey are far superior in technology in every way to the other panels I was paying more for because they were smaller Solar panels rarely produce their stated wattage. The sun changes strength throughout the day and there will be clouds. Use only real world data for power calculations.Solar panels cost generally $100+ for 100W I have 2.4kw in a forest.For batteries, figure out how much you use and then plan on running off batteries for 48 hours. Your solar ray should be able to charge these batteries in a day or two if possible. Whatever you do, remain power positive so that you will always be charging batteries when sunny. I have 9KWH LiFePO4.Always keep at least two batteries connected to spread the load and so always purchase 24 volt or less with these. Unless of course you're trying to charge a car. I am not. These batteries can charge and drain 90% instead of half like the rest. 200AH will cost $500+ mine is 800AH.This just leaves the charge controller. $250-$300. I have never had one that didn't work fine no matter what was going on at 48 volts. MPPT on anything but a well pump.Mine was $450 but it can power a small village. You will also need hundreds of dollars of cables.Just to leave you with a few solid numbers. Heavy side on the electronics 200 to 400W. World's smallest air conditioner or heater 500W.Multiply Watt by time used per device to find draw.#Hurricane #Solar #Asheville #Helene#HurricaneHurricane#SolarSolar#AshevilleAsheville#HeleneHelene">
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="0" value="2024-10-07 15:33:29+00:00" />
          <attvalue for="1" value="B_Whitewind@regenerate.social" />
          <attvalue for="2" value="4" />
          <attvalue for="3" value="0" />
          <attvalue for="4" value="3" />
          <attvalue for="5" value="root" />
          <attvalue for="6" value="https://regenerate.social/@B_Whitewind/113266865573256842" />
          <attvalue for="7" value="1.0" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
