      <node id="113258230189180548" label="ed areas with web access should check their state and local government sites for cleanup guidance; some areas will get assistance on specific dates—don’t miss out; some areas will have specific rules for free assistance (such as not taking debris with containers of hazardous waste or ACs &amp; refrigerators with freon mixed in; some will ask you to put debris in the street after a certain date or tell you when boxes will be left on your block for you to fill; 6) FEMA &amp; the CDC have good general guidelines that are easy to find; for people who don’t trust them, try the American Red Cross https://www.redcross.org/get-help/disaster-relief-and-recovery-services/disaster-cleanup.html ; 7) if you have family without internet access and you’re going to help, print out any specific guidelines for their town or county and take them along in a plastic bag (the Red Cross guidelines will tell you what else to take—they can’t have too many garbage bags, sanitary supplies, or cleaners) 2/x #helene@ai6yr@ai6yrai6yrhttps://www.redcross.org/get-help/disaster-relief-and-recovery-services/disaster-cleanup.htmlhttps://www.redcross.org/get-help/disaster-relief-and-recovery-services/disaster-cleanup.html#helenehelene">
