      <node id="113256437507684793" label="the water, but clean water has been restored at the houses at the bottom of the mountain, and businesses (and restaurants) along the main road are open. Still no landline phones or broadband. (As a reference point for some of the hardest hit areas, this is about 15 miles south.)Some people in the area have hit their breaking point. There was an active shooter in Hendersonville. (He was detained—I think before anyone was hurt.) Another neighbor (not the same one that I mention a few days ago, who was threatened in his car) is in a lot of pain from a black eye and bruised jaw. A guy in the car behind him beat him up because he wasn’t inching forward fast enough in traffic. But up on the mountain above, it is quiet and calm and neighbors are looking out for each other.As long as their physical health doesn’t require any emergency services, it’s mostly my parents’ mental health that I’m concerned about. They will say everything is fine even if it’s not—especially because so many others nearby have it so much worse. I’ve had an upcoming trip booked to visit them since well before the storm was forecast. And my parents are really looking forward to it. A week from now, I’ll be there, and I’ll pack to stay beyond my planned return if it looks like they need me.#Helene#HeleneHelene">
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="0" value="2024-10-05 19:21:29+00:00" />
          <attvalue for="1" value="alysonsee@sunny.garden" />
          <attvalue for="2" value="1" />
          <attvalue for="3" value="1" />
          <attvalue for="4" value="1" />
          <attvalue for="5" value="reply" />
          <attvalue for="6" value="https://sunny.garden/@alysonsee/113256437486605955" />
          <attvalue for="7" value="2.71" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="113249410311320989" label="ble to do laundry, go out to eat and a movie, and pick up supplies for themselves, the neighbors, and the food kitchen. Because they had TV at the motel, they finally got to see the pictures of just how complete and widespread the devastation is. (As one person explained it, imagine 20 feet of water gushing through a narrow valley for continuously for 24 hours. That’s why whole towns are gone.) In phone calls with them this week, they each expressed survivor’s guilt. They are headed home today. The power company is making progress and says that they will have electricity restored by 9:45 PM. And the homes at the bottom of the mountain have clean water. I’m still the main source of local news and internet searches for them and for neighbors. I hung out on the phone last night with one neighbor while he tried to fix his generator. I texted with another who cares for her husband with Alzheimer’s. (Imagine being in the middle of all of this, alone at home, with a spouse who doesn’t recognize you and is a danger to himself.)My 80+ yo Mom is going to volunteer at the food bank this weekend, assembling packets of instant oatmeal and dehydrated milk for distribution. #Helene#HeleneHelene">
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="0" value="2024-10-04 13:34:22+00:00" />
