      <node id="113232360138016104" label="t only did they find a working truck stop about 60 miles south, they booked my parents a room there for a few nights. This should help a lot with Dad’s cabin fever. He cannot manage his nervous energy and keeps taking the truck out to ride around, much to the dismay of everyone who cares about him and about the greater good. (Another neighbor tried to go to a store to get a replacement battery and got stuck in a long line of traffic. When he tried to turn around, some guys in a long line of cars queued up in the other direction for a gas station assumed he was trying to cut in front of them and jumped out and threatened to mess him up and started rocking his vehicle.)I found a video online from someone who lives in their area, and their situation is much like my parents’: No power but also no major damage. Meanwhile, down at the bottom of the mountain, although the road is intact, buildings were flooded out. Still, this is nothing like the total devastation 15 miles north and extending across a 45-mile by 45-mile band north of there, which would warrant a CW to repeat what I’ve heard. Emergency workers are tired and have PTSD from the things they have seen and heard.#Helene #NCwx#HeleneHelene#NCwxNCwx">
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="0" value="2024-10-01 13:18:18+00:00" />
          <attvalue for="1" value="alysonsee@sunny.garden" />
