      <node id="113204473595670761" label="T 105 MPH12H  27/0000Z 28.8N  84.3W  105 KT 120 MPH24H  27/1200Z 33.9N  84.5W   55 KT  65 MPH...INLANDAfter initial state (0700EST), the lines before and after landing  (1900EST ish) show a remarkable drop in wind speed, which means a BIG dispersion of energy.Storm is also moving LINEAR relatively fast, 15-25mph NNE.All caveats here: A fast linear storm with a big vortex sound bad, but that means less exposure to a single spot, at any given time, and energy dispersed along a big area. The worst scenario is a cyclone with a perfect round eye, a very well defined, SMALL vortex, moving slowly linear. Think Andrew, 1992.#Helene #Florida#HeleneHelene#FloridaFlorida">
