      <node id="113358711770881791" label="t. 15-16). Renown worldwide as the site of an alleged alien spacecraft crash in 1947, Roswell is an impressive city that serves as the economic focal point of Southeast New Mexico. Roswell, New Mexicoalien spacecraft crash in 1947Little did we know that within days of our departure the city would be devastated by flash floods. These arose from getting nearly six inches of heavy rain in a matter of hours from severe thunderstorms on Saturday (Oct. 19) evening/night.The rainfall total broke an 123 year-old record and caused streams to rapidly overflow their banks in the heart of the city and throughout the surrounding area. At one point, every route into or out of the city was blocked. Places we had walked a mere 72 hours prior were inundated with rapidly moving water, carrying debris, vehicles, and people with it.We had parked our car at the convention center lot on Wednesday. Saturday night, it was completely under water and people in the center itself had to rush to the rooftop as rampaging waters burst into the building. They were among the hundreds that needed rescuing that tragic and terrifying night. The Roswell Museum Center across the normally dry North Spring River from the convention center has been closed this week due to the storm. It is hard to imagine the extent damage that may have happened there to the exhibits, which includes a full recreation of Robert H. Goddard’s rocket workshop.  Roswell Museum CenterRobert H. Goddard’s rocket workshop**An October 24, 2024 update regarding the museum is provided in the box below**An October 24, 2024 update regarding the museum is provided in the box below**“City Officials said the Roswell Museum sustained at least $12 million in water damage and that pieces of artwork were being sent out of state to be repaired.”Source: citydesk.org“City Officials said the Roswell Museum sustained at least $12 million in water damage and that pieces of artwork were being sent out of state to be repaired.”Source: citydesk.orgIt is almost unfathomable to comprehend the intensity of this storm that left two dead and so much devastation in its wake. At the same time, it is unfathomable to imagine Roswell just ceding its future to this deadly storm.This High Plains city is home to a hardy bunch of New Mexicans who literally “weather” erratic climatic variations all the time. From drastic temperature changes to dust storms to blizzards out of nowhere, they’ve endured it all. And following the tragic events of Saturday night, they will pick up, clean up, repair, rebuild, and persevere. Having been so impressed by the good people of Roswell during our visit, one would expect nothing less.Please consider sending a donation to one of the following organizations to help aid in the recovery and rebuilding efforts in Roswell, New Mexico.Albuquerque Community Foundation, Emergency Action Fund for the Roswell FloodCommunity Foundation of Southern New Mexico, the Greatest Needs Impact FundAlbuquerque Community Foundation, Emergency Action Fund for the Roswell FloodAlbuquerque Community Foundation, Emergency Action Fund for the Roswell FloodCommunity Foundation of Southern New Mexico, the Greatest Needs Impact FundCommunity Foundation of Southern New Mexico, the Greatest Needs Impact FundUpdates on cleanup progress, debris removal, and repairs are available on the city’s Facebook page. city’s Facebook pagePeace! https://panethos.wordpress.com/2024/10/23/visiting-roswell-just-days-before-disaster/https://panethos.wordpress.com/2024/10/23/visiting-roswell-just-days-before-disaster/#aliens #cities #environment #flooding #floods #geography #history #landUse #NewMexico #planning #Roswell #storms #travel #weather#aliens#cities#environment#flooding#floods#geography#history#landUse#NewMexico#planning#Roswell#storms#travel#weather">
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="0" value="2024-10-23 20:50:59+00:00" />
          <attvalue for="1" value="panethos.wordpress.com@panethos.wordpress.com" />
          <attvalue for="2" value="0" />
          <attvalue for="3" value="0" />
          <attvalue for="4" value="0" />
